    {
      "id": "persistence_recovery",
      "scenario": "Write and acknowledge an event, fail partway through write/flush, restart from a torn UTF-8 tail, then recover cancelled work and redeliver identified input.",
      "decision": "refactor",
      "reason": "The writer retains its first JSONL failure, suppresses subsequent writes/projections and reports failure on later flushes. Only complete successful records reach DuckDB. Reader and writer agree on the newline commit boundary; startup removes only the ignored tail before append. Appender owns commit acknowledgement and recovery stops for a session whose fixup cannot commit. Streaming and panic diagnostics keep best-effort asynchronous persistence; DuckDB stays rebuildable.",
      "validation": [
        "Write and flush fault injection formerly produced false success and projection-only records; both regressions now pass.",
        "Real-file cases cover a long partial tail, an unterminated valid record, and a split UTF-8 character while preserving complete corrupt lines and sequence numbering.",
        "The real daemon boots from a corrupt line plus split-UTF-8 tail and saves the next user/assistant exchange. Existing killed/drained-daemon replay and original-turn-ID tests pass."
      ],
